# Relational Algebra

This document summarizes the relational-algebra component of the DM505 Database Design examination.

The exercise involved translating between relational-algebra expressions and SQL, as well as translating SQL statements into extended relational algebra.

## Relational Algebra to SQL

### Query A

```sql
SELECT *
FROM T, R
WHERE T.B > T.C;
```

### Query B

```sql
(SELECT B, D FROM S)
EXCEPT ALL
(SELECT C, A FROM R);
```

### Query C

```sql
(SELECT *
 FROM T NATURAL LEFT OUTER JOIN S)

INTERSECT ALL

(SELECT *
 FROM R NATURAL RIGHT OUTER JOIN S);
```

### Query D

```sql
SELECT *
FROM R, S
WHERE C > D;
```

### Query E

```sql
SELECT
    B,
    C,
    SUM(A)
FROM T
NATURAL LEFT OUTER JOIN S
GROUP BY B, C;
```

---

## SQL to Extended Relational Algebra

The second part of the exercise involved extended relational-algebra expressions using:

- projection
- conditional joins
- outer joins
- duplicate elimination
- grouping
- aggregation

The submitted expressions included:

```text
π[R.A, S.B, C, D] (S RIGHT-OUTER-JOIN[C=D AND R.A>S.A] R)

δ(π[B] (T JOIN[C=3 OR D=3] R))

γ[S.A, SUM(T.C)] (S JOIN[S.B=T.B] T)
```

In the original submission, a notation clarification was included for the first expression: the arrow used there was intended to denote a **right outer join**.
